== Ecology ==
''Hypericum foliosum'' is [[Endemism|endemic]] to the Azores Islands in the North Atlantic,<ref name=":1" /> where it can be found on every island.{{Sfn|Robson|1985|p=300}} It inhabits the laurel and juniper forests of the archipelago,{{Sfn|Santos|Figueiredo|Barroso|Pedro|1999|p=283}} especially in shady and damp areas of mountainous regions at elevations of {{Convert|220-800|m|sp=us}}.{{Sfn|Robson|1985|p=300}} It also grows in stands of ''[[Pittosporum]]'' trees and is quick to inhabit [[volcanic ash]] deposits.{{Sfn|Santos|Figueiredo|Barroso|Pedro|1999|p=283}} In general, it is good at colonizing recently cleared areas like man-made clearings and landslide sites. Populations at a site are usually made up of only a few plants.{{Sfn|Moura|1998|p=244}}

Several parasites are hosted by the leaves of ''H.&nbsp;foliosum''. The [[Rust (fungus)|rust fungus]] ''[[Melampsora hypericorum]]'' takes the form of a pustule on the leaf, damaging the surface. [[Larva]]e of the moth ''[[Caloptilia aurantiaca]]'' mine into the leaves and later live under the folded tip of the blade.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hypericum foliosum – Plant Parasites of Europe |url=https://bladmineerders.nl/host-plants/plantae/spermatopsida/angiosperma/eudicots/superrosids/rosids/fabids/malpighiales/hypericaceae/hypericum/hypericum-foliosum/ |access-date=2023-12-05 |website=bladmineerders.nl}}</ref>

''H. foliosum'' is not directly endangered by competition with [[invasive species]] or human actions;{{Sfn|Santos|Figueiredo|Barroso|Pedro|1999|p=283}} it was listed as [[Least-concern species|Least Concern]] by the [[IUCN Red List|IUCN]] in 2016.<ref name="iucn status 19 November 2021"/> However, it has been studied as a model for using [[micropropagation]] as a conservation method on the Azores. Micropropagation has previously been used in ''[[Hypericum perforatum]]'' and ''[[Hypericum canariense]]''. A single node of the species that is propagated using this method can yield 2–4 new plants in around four months.{{Sfn|Moura|1998|p=248}}

== Uses ==
While no official [[Pharmacology|pharmacological]] usage of ''Hypericum foliosum'' were recorded as of 2011,{{Sfn|Rainha|Lima|Baptista|Rodrigues|2011|p=1933}} locals in the Azores describe the species as being used in [[traditional medicine]] in similar ways to other species in the genus.{{Sfn|Rainha|Lima|Baptista|Rodrigues|2011|p=1931}} Some of these applications derive from the [[diuretic]], [[Hepatoprotection|hepatoprotective]], and [[Antihypertensive drug|antihypertensive]] properties of its extract.{{Sfn|Caldeira|Zhang|Gouveia|Videira|2023|p=1087}} Despite having more phenolic activity than ''[[Hypericum undulatum|H.&nbsp;undulatum]]'' and ''[[Hypericum androsaemum|H.&nbsp;androsaemum]]'', those two species are as or more common in Portuguese medicinal markets.{{Sfn|Rainha|Lima|Baptista|Rodrigues|2011|p=1931}} ''H.&nbsp;foliosum'' has exhibited ''[[in vitro]]'' [[antibiotic]] effects on infectious bacteria like ''[[Staphylococcus aureus]].''{{Sfn|Gibbons|Moser|Hausmann|Stavri|2005|p=1272}} Its carotenoid and phenolic oils give the plant's extract antioxidant properties. The lower parts of the plant like the stem, bark, and roots are the most effective.{{Sfn|Rainha|Lima|Baptista|Rodrigues|2011|p=1938}}

